    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sheep Mountain anticline, Wyoming, is an asymmetric fold resulting from Laramide compression accommodated by an underlying basement-cored thrust fault. The structure has excellent surface exposures, and has been the subject of many studies in recent decades. These studies provide considerable background knowledge pertaining to the geologic history and kinematic evolution of the structure, a wealth of quality data (particularly fracture mapping by Bellahsen, et al. (2006a) and Fiore (2006)), and numerous unanswered questions. We will construct an enhanced 3-dimensional structural model of the fold using a high resolution LiDAR topographic map supplemented by field data and the structural interpretation of others. Using this model, we intend to apply a commercial finite element code which includes frictional contact algorithms, multiple constitutive laws, and stress and displacement boundary conditions to further investigate 3-dimensional relationships between fault geometry, fold geometry, and fracture patterns, as well as the mechanical effects of discrete stratigraphic units with highly variable strengths and stiffnesses and which may deform according to different constitutive laws.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
